-Runtime findings and image assessments are detection signals. Human-reviewed changes through Git and Flux remain the remediation path; the agent does not terminate or isolate arbitrary workloads.
+### Scope, capacity and security
+
+The proposed node-agent cohort is exactly `metal3` and `metal6`. The pinned v0.3.310 image, detector rules, environment, host mounts, bounded eBPF capabilities and 100m/180Mi requests with 500m/1400Mi limits remain unchanged. Required affinity restricts the DaemonSet template to those two workers; admission also checks the per-node affinity generated by the DaemonSet controller. Agent kubelet proxy reads extend only to those two node names. The collector has no node-proxy access. Other Kubescape workloads retain restricted admission controls; PSA/Kyverno remain enforced. No other six nodes or production application scheduling changes are included.
+
+The read-only capacity observation found `metal3` Ready without pressure and matching the accepted `metal6` Talos v1.12.8, kernel 6.18.32-talos, containerd 2.2.4 and amd64 combination. At the retained sample, `metal3` used about 659m CPU and 3,535Mi memory against 3,950m/7,172Mi allocatable. Existing requests were 1,609m/2,960Mi; adding the agent requests gives 1,709m/3,140Mi. Its complete 1,400Mi memory ceiling would still leave about 2,237Mi at that sample. Existing workload limits already overcommit the node; these samples are neither simultaneous peak reservations nor sustained-load acceptance. The observed existing agent used about 35m/192Mi. Preserve its limits and supervise pressure, throttling, OOMs and restart counts after rollout. Metal3-specific eBPF initialization and peak overhead remain pending; matching versions do not prove those live gates.
+
+The collector remains one non-root, read-only-root, RuntimeDefault-seccomp process with the existing Recreate strategy and dedicated 256Mi Longhorn PVC. A projected rotating token is reread per GET with CA/hostname verification and no redirects/proxies. Namespace Roles permit only exact reviewed agent Pod metadata/logs, named Homepage Deployments, required ReplicaSets and Pod inventory. There are no Secrets, arbitrary Pod logs, exec/attach, node-proxy or unrelated-namespace permissions. Cilium enforcement stays `never`; no NetworkPolicy isolation is claimed. Scanner deployments, vulnerability CronJobs and history storage are untouched; `assessment_history_available=false` keeps `history_unavailable` explicit. There are no GitHub credentials, network publishing client, automatic response or automatic closure. Gatus collector checks have empty alert lists.
+
+### Multi-source state and coverage
+
+Each source verifies the exact DaemonSet UID/template image, Pod name/UID, node, ServiceAccount, running image, container ID and restart count before and after reading its logs. Event attribution separately rereads Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod ownership and running image identity; the event's Pod UID, container ID, digest, owner claims and observation time must match. Missing/deleted/unenrolled owners produce only sanitized unknown source observations, never fabricated incidents. Conflicting attribution is rejected. Normalized private proofs retain the verified controller/Pod/container/source/digest context; raw command lines and process payloads are discarded.
+
+The additive SQLite migration creates `runtime_source_status`, `runtime_identities` and `runtime_evidence`; it never renames, deletes or resets legacy tables or `runtime/metal6`. Per-node cursors, normalized queues and accepted identity proofs commit together. Source audit permits at most four previously unseen identities per hour and 128 retained identities per node. Evidence has a shared 20,000-record ceiling. Bounds fail visibly; they never evict evidence to make room. Production retains historical receipts and incident history, including beyond the existing seven-day ingestion window. Existing queue, incident and 64Mi database bounds remain; capacity needs reviewed archival rather than an automatic reset. SQLite locking and Recreate preserve the single writer.
+
+One failed source leaves the other source polling. Coverage counts a workload only when **all** observed ready replicas have immutable running digests on nodes with currently verified agents and successful authenticated reads. Mixed covered/uncovered replicas, missing inventory and moves to other nodes reduce the actual count; the collector never relocates applications. Retained gaps are reported independently from current instrumented/collecting coverage. A successful later read never clears a retained gap. The intended count is 2/2 for the Homepage deployments while their actual replicas remain on `metal3` and that source is enrolled and healthy.
+
+Private mode-0600 `status.json`, `previews.json` and SQLite distinguish process/storage health, expected/healthy agents, each node's last successful read and reason, invalid identity counts, coverage by workload/replica/digest, event/attributed counts, queue/database health, gaps and unavailable assessments. HTTP exposes aggregates only. `/readyz` measures loop/storage progress; `/healthz` also requires all expected sources, full coverage, qualified assessments and no retained gaps. Gatus retains separate process and source checks; source health is expected to stay degraded for unavailable history and historical gaps even after 2/2 current coverage. Neither a Ready DaemonSet Pod nor one healthy node establishes overall acceptance.
+
+### Reviewed staged enrollment
+
+**Operator-approved change in scope:** keep exact Pod-name RBAC, then enroll after rollout through a reviewed GitOps configuration change. A new DaemonSet Pod name is generated by Kubernetes and cannot be pinned before it exists. [RBAC resourceNames](https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/rbac/#referring-to-resources) cannot express an owner/node selector. No new privileged role generator or unrestricted log grant is introduced.
+
+1. Independently review the PR head and require green Repo Checks, Kubescape Assessment Checks and Security Collector Image jobs. The existing image workflow rebuilds the deterministic layer, checks the retained proof and deployment digest, exercises process restrictions, publishes that exact image and requires anonymous digest retrieval. Do not merge if publication or anonymous retrieval fails. No CI success is inferred from local builds.
+2. The operator approves and merges the rollout. Verify the Flux revision and policy/HelmRelease readiness, two agent Pods only on the cohort, source image identities, eBPF/trace_exec/container-watcher initialization and runtime binding refresh. Verify resources, no pressure/OOM/restarts, one collector, its existing PVC UID/Bound state and three healthy Longhorn replicas. Do not run the live R1000 test yet. A material metal3 compatibility/capacity failure stops acceptance and triggers the reviewed rollback below.
+3. The new metal3 source initially reports `source_enrollment_pending`, zero coverage on that node and no log attempt. A DaemonSet template rollout may also replace the previously pinned metal6 Pod; its stale pin reports failure and preserves its cursor. This temporary degradation is intentional and must not be reported as 2/2 coverage.
+4. In a clean checkout, use the operator's explicit cooked-k8s context to prepare local pins:
+
+   ```sh
+   python3 ops/security-collector/enroll-agents.py --context entra@homelab --proof /tmp/reviewed-agent-identities.json
+   git diff -- infra/kubescape/collector
+   ```
+
+   The helper performs bounded Kubernetes GETs only. It rejects missing/ambiguous candidates, a changed pinned DaemonSet UID, wrong owner/ServiceAccount/node/image or an identity changing between two independent observations. It prepares both configured source pins and only their exact `resourceNames`; it never applies resources, reads logs or runs the test. Retain the proof for private review. A reviewer independently verifies these observations and the scoped RBAC diff, then merges that configuration PR. Flux restarts the collector on the same PVC. If an agent changes before reconciliation, repeat the observation and review rather than widening permissions.
+5. Verify advancing per-node reads, two healthy authenticated sources and the actual 2/2 workload coverage. On a container restart within the pinned Pod, the collector rechecks the identity/image and records the changed container in its bounded audit with a retained gap. A replacement Pod requires repeating step 4; it is never automatically trusted. Repeated flapping hits the visible refresh limit; do not clear the audit to bypass it. Verify denied permissions with authorization introspection for Secrets, arbitrary Pod logs, exec/attach, node proxy and workload writes. Preserve all source errors and old evidence.
+
